%! Rules that can never fire.
%!
%! Nemo infers the type of every column from the declarations, the facts
%! and the rules. If a rule body requires a value that a column can never
%! contain, the rule can never fire. Nemo warns about such rules before
%! reasoning starts.
%!
%! The example shows:
%! - how declarations give types to the columns of predicates,
%! - how Nemo finds rules that can never fire.

@declare person(name: string, age: int) .
@declare city(name: string, location: (int, int)) .

person("Alice", 30) .
person("Bob", 25) .
person("Charlie", 20.0) .
city("Dresden", (51, 13)) .

adult(?name) :- person(?name, ?age), ?age >= 18 .

% A name is a string and an age is an integer, so ?x cannot be both:
nameAndAge(?x) :- person(?x, _), person(_, ?x) .

% A location is a pair, so it never matches a pattern for triples:
wide(?a) :- city(_, (?a, _, _)) .

@output adult, nameAndAge, wide .
